<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Red Vienna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1919">1919</a>–<a href="/years/1934">1934</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Rotes Wien</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Red <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> was the period from 1919 to 1934 when the Social Democrats governed the city of Vienna. Funded by a new housing tax, the city built over 60,000 council flats in large estates such as the Karl-Marx-Hof, together with schools, clinics, baths and kindergartens. The experiment was ended in February 1934, when the Dollfuss government crushed the Social Democrats in the Austrian Civil War.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/red-vienna.jpg" alt="Red Vienna" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dreizung · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karl-Marx-Hof_2009.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-saint-germain-en-laye-1919">Treaty of Saint-Germain-en-Laye</a> forbade any union of the new Austrian republic with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and left it with little territory and limited access to the Hungarian breadbasket that had fed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> for decades. The middle classes, many holding worthless war bonds, were plunged into poverty by hyperinflation, apartments were overcrowded, and tuberculosis, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-flu">Spanish flu</a> and syphilis raged. People elsewhere in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> called the capital Wasserkopf, big head, a city too big for so small a country.</p><p>In the municipal elections of 4 May 1919, the first in Austrian history in which all adult citizens of both sexes could vote, the Social Democratic Workers' Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, the SDAP, won a majority and Jakob Reumann became mayor. Nationally it won only 40.8 per cent of the vote, and after its coalition with the Christian Social Party broke down in 1920 it never again led the federal government. The two parties agreed to separate Red Vienna from Black Lower Austria, and on 1 January 1922 Vienna became the ninth federal state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>From 1925 to 1934 more than 60,000 apartments were built in municipal blocks round green courts, as at the Karl-Marx-Hof, housing as many as 200,000 people, a tenth of the population. Tenants were chosen by a ranking that gave priority to people with disabilities and other vulnerable groups. The Housing Tax paid forty per cent of the costs and the rest came from the Luxury Tax and federal funds. The finance councillor Hugo Breitner refused to borrow and taxed luxuries such as riding horses, large private cars, servants and hotel rooms.</p><p>The health councillor Julius Tandler built up welfare services. By 1931 the city had handed out 53,000 clothing packages to parents in need; kindergartens grew fivefold, schools gave subsidised lunches and free medical and dental examinations, and public baths were built. Infant mortality fell below half its pre-war level. The Feuerhalle Simmering crematorium, opened in 1923, set the city against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ignaz-seipel">Ignaz Seipel</a>'s national government, and in 1924 the Constitutional Court sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Much of the city's welfare relied on federal money, and in the early thirties the federal government began to starve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> of funds, forcing cuts. The First Republic collapsed in 1934, a year after Bundeskanzler Engelbert Dollfuß had suspended the Nationalrat, and the banning of the SDAP ended the project. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-polanyi">Karl Polanyi</a> wrote in 1944 that Vienna had achieved one of the most spectacular cultural triumphs of Western history. Many of the housing complexes survive.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Red Vienna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The republic was polarised between the Christian Social Party, strong in the countryside, and the Social Democrats of <b>Red Vienna</b>, who built large council housing estates.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-austrian-republic">First Austrian Republic</a></li></ul></section>
